A||||7||Bill 88||starting||||1||
Z||000000||Bill 88||08-26-2001||07:41 PM|||| OK, I checked all the post for this problem but I have not found anything.  My 88 4cyl won't start.  I turn the key and it just clicks.  The voltage at the battery is 11.34v, but my scanner says the voltage is 10.2v.  I have cleanned the battery cables, redid the ground on the block, retightened the starter wire and checked the other grounds I could reach.  Any help will be appreciated.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Kasdan||08-26-2001||10:27 PM||||Starter bad?||||reg||1
Z||000002||ocalafiero1||08-26-2001||10:34 PM||||Take the starter out and have it tested at a local parts place. One other thing you might want to check is (If your car has it) that any metallic insulating tape over the wire loom is not shorting out against any part of the starter or selonoid.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocd||08-26-2001||11:10 PM||||I had the same problem with my BMW and my friends Ranger.  I checked the battery voltage and it was 12.38.  I couldn't figure out why the thing wouldn't start so I took it to the dealer.  The mechanic looked at it for about 5 minutes, then told me my battery was shot.  I didn't believe him but when I put a new battery in, it worked fine.  It had something to do with the cranking amps I think.  Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||08-26-2001||11:50 PM||||Any battery can read 12v or more but not have enough reserve cranking amps (charge) to do anything with. That's why they should be checked under load or with a specific gravity checker. Your headlights are probable dim too. May just need charging, but summer heat is murder on a battery.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||08-27-2001||12:32 AM||||If it's reading 11.34 with no load your battery is low. What does the voltage drop to when you try to start it? It could be a bad battery or could just need charging. You should check the alternator output after you get it going to make sure it's not getting weak and letting the battery go down.||||reg||1
Z||000006||V8Dreamer||08-27-2001||02:21 PM||||I'm goin with the bad battery theme......I've had to replace 3 batteries in the last year,  especially in Fieros since the battery goes through such a harsh range of temps.  When they die, as long as they aren't completely dead yet, the starter will click rather than turning the engine over, replace the battery and it'll probably crank right over.  If you don't wanna dish out the dough til you're sure it's the battery, try jumping it!<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000007||Spektrum-87GT||08-27-2001||07:04 PM||||i had the same type of problem with my 91 jeep cherokee. turn the key... and... 'click'<BR>sometimes i was lucky and got a 'click, click, click, click'<BR>take your starter down to autozone and have it checked. also check all the battery cables and the grounds. My problem ended up being both the power cables AND the starter. hope this helps.||||reg||1
